course: dictionary
lesson: 15985
title: Formal words for everyday things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound grown-up because they come from science or medicine. Urination is one of them. Its everyday partner is «going for a wee». Both name the same thing, but you would hear each one in a different place.
explain: Urination is a noun for an action. It comes from the verb urinate, which means to pass urine out of the body. You cannot count urination like apples, so it is uncountable, like water or sand.
explain: Science lessons use this word when they study the body. «The kidneys clean the blood and make urine.» The urine is stored in the bladder and then released by urination. Look at the words around a new word, and the context will tell you what it is about.
explain: Doctors also use the word. «The nurse asked Sam about his urination.» The nurse is not being rude: she wants to check that his body is working well. Context tells you the word is about health, not about manners.
fact: Urination means passing urine (wee) out of the body.
fact: The kidneys clean the blood and make urine.
fact: Urine is stored in the bladder until it is released by urination.
fact: «Urination» is a formal, science word; «going for a wee» is everyday talk.
fact: Doctors may ask about urination to check a person's health.
exercise: «The kidneys make urine, and urination releases it from the body.» What does urination mean? | Drinking plenty of water | Cleaning the blood with a sponge | *Passing urine out of the body | Storing food in the stomach
exercise: In everyday talk, what is another way to say urination? | Going to sleep | *Going for a wee | Eating lunch | Taking a bath
exercise: «The urine is stored in the bladder before urination.» Which organ stores the urine? | Lungs | *Bladder | Liver | Heart
test: Which organ makes urine by cleaning the blood? | Brain | *Kidneys | Heart | Stomach
test: Ella's science book says «urination is the act of passing urine out of the body». Is urination a formal or everyday word? | Slang from the playground | A kind of animal | A name for a food | *Formal, from science
exam: A doctor asks a patient about urination. Why might she ask? | *To check that the body is healthy and working well | To measure how tall the patient is | Because it is dinner time | To find out the patient's favourite colour
